<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herat is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, historically a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, especially in the fifteenth century, it became a capital and a leading centre of Persian art, literature and scholarship. It was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat-school">Herat school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herat.jpg" alt="Herat" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yves Picq · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herat_7452a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Herat lies in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, south of the Paropamisus Mountains in the fertile valley of the Hari River. It stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between West Asia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and was traditionally known for its wine. The city and its district take their ancient name from the river: under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> (550-330 BCE) the district was called Haraiva, and classical sources call the region Areia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The date of Herat's foundation is unknown. At the time of the Arab invasion in the middle of the 7th century, Sasanian power in the province was largely nominal, and Hephthalite tribal lords had settled in the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> and some Turks opposed the invaders at the battle of Qohestān in 651-52 but were defeated. In the Middle Ages Herat was one of the important cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, known as the Pearl of Khorasan. In 994 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-tegin">Alptegin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> defeated its rebellious governor Faik and annexed Herat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> and Tous.</p><p>After its conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> the city became an important centre of intellectual and artistic life in the Islamic world. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a> it was the focal point of the Timurid Renaissance, whose grandeur is thought to have matched that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in the Italian Renaissance. It then remained part of Safavid Iran until the early 18th century. In 1716 the Abdali Afghans in the city revolted and set up the Sadozai Sultanate of Herat, and in 1732 it became part of Afsharid Iran. Ahmad Shah Durrani took it in 1747. In the first half of the 19th century it was an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> and faced several invasions from Qajar Iran. It was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Herat is the capital of Herat Province and a major industrial and trading centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Most of its people speak Dari, a form of Persian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a> is the second major language. Its historic sites include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friday-mosque-of-herat">Great Mosque of Herat</a>, the Herat Citadel and the Musalla Complex.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Timur marched from <b>Herat</b>, Shah Wali and his sons were assassinated at Farah, Sulaiman surrendered, and Timur took the throne in November 1772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ghor, a remote and mountainous region of central Afghanistan between <b>Herat</b> and Ghazni, was among the last parts of the eastern Iranian world to accept Islam, and its people lived in fortified villages under local chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They may have built fortified grid-plan cities at Bukhara, Panjikent and <b>Herat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the east, Qajar attempts to recover <b>Herat</b>, once part of the Safavid realm, failed: Mohammad Shah besieged it in vain in 1837–1838, and when Naser al-Din Shah took it in 1856 Britain declared war and forced Iran to renounce its claims in the Treaty of Paris of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The cities kept their crafts: Tabriz was a centre of artists and calligraphers, a group of whom Baysunghur carried off to <b>Herat</b> in 1421.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>With the reformed army Abbas recovered <b>Herat</b> and Khorasan from the Uzbeks in 1598, retook Tabriz and Azerbaijan from the Ottomans between 1603 and 1607, expelled the Portuguese from Hormuz with English naval help in 1622, and seized Baghdad and most of Iraq in 1623–1624.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
